/** Translation maps for siman 062 — imported by _patch-siman-062.mjs */
export const TRANSLATIONS = {
  mechaber: {
    '1#main': `Laws of eivar min hachai (limb from a living animal). In it are 4 seifim: A limb from a living animal is forbidden in domesticated animal, undomesticated animal, and fowl.`,
    '2#main': `A limb that separated from a living animal is forbidden; and flesh that separated from a living animal is forbidden because of "flesh in the field you shall not eat" (treifah). Even if it separated from the animal and is still inside it — such as if a piece was cut from the spleen or kidneys and the cut piece remained inside — it is forbidden.`,
    '3#main': `A limb or flesh that is dangling — if it cannot return and live, even if it did not separate from the animal until after slaughter — it is forbidden. {Rama: And see above siman 55: the cauda equina standing in a place that does not render the animal treifah is permitted to eat, and we do not say the extra portion is considered as removed and is like a dangling limb (Or Zarua, HaArukh). A limb that slipped from its place in a place that does not render the animal treifah is permitted to eat it even if skin and flesh do not cover most of it (Rashba siman 69). But the custom is to be stringent also in this (Or Zarua, HaArukh).}`,
    '4#main': `Male eggs that were plucked and are still wrapped in their sac are not forbidden from the Torah, for there is in them a measure of life-force; therefore they do not rot; nevertheless it is forbidden to eat them — from the custom that Israel practiced not to eat them because they resemble a limb from a living animal.`,
  },
  'siftei-kohen': {
    '1#א': `["<b>Forbidden.</b> And one receives lashes for it — so is explicit in the Talmud and poskim:"]`,
    '1#ב': `["<b>And fowl.</b> But not in fish and locusts, as was explained above at the beginning of siman 13:"]`,
    '2#א': `["<b>A limb that separated from a living animal.</b> Whether it has flesh, sinews, and bones, or whether it has only flesh alone — such as the tongue, spleen, kidneys, and eggs — it is forbidden to eat it, end of his words — Tur; and so too the Rambam at the beginning of chapter 5 of Forbidden Foods; and Rav HaMagid wrote there that so is explained in the sugya of the chapter of the displaced sinew, and it is agreed by all the commentators there; and it is implied from their words there that lashes apply also in this; and so is implied in the Talmud there. Further, the Tur wrote that eivar min hachai applies only in pure domesticated animal, undomesticated animal, and fowl, and not in impure ones — and this is explicit in the Talmud and all the poskim; and the Mechaber omitted all this because he follows his reasoning, for he challenged in his book Beit Yosef on the Tur that he inserted himself to write these laws, since for us there is no practical difference in this, for in the end they are forbidden in any case; but Bach wrote that there is a practical difference where it is forbidden because of eivar min hachai — it is a complete entity and is not nullified, as below siman 100; and his words are correct in this. But that which Bach wrote at the end of his words — and these are his words: and see that in pure animals, where there is eivar min hachai, it is forbidden to give it to children of Noah because of lifnei iver, do not place a stumbling block; for a child of Noah is commanded regarding eivar min hachai; and in impure animals it is permitted to give — end of his words; and so too in Hagahot Perishah — and these are his words: and our teacher says it does not apply in impure animals, that one may give it to him l'chatchila, for because of impure animal one does not transgress, since a child of Noah is not commanded regarding it; and eivar min hachai does not apply to him — one cannot say that a child of Noah transgresses eivar min hachai also in an impure animal, for is there anything that is permitted for Israel and forbidden for an idolater — end of his words — I did not grasp the end of his words, for behold in chapter 3 (daf 102) it is in the entire sugya and in the baraita of the dispute regarding Israel; but regarding a child of Noah all agree he is warned regarding impure animals; and so it is in the chapter of the hide and the sauce (Chullin daf 121b) in the entire sugya, see there; and so wrote the Rambam chapter 9 of Laws of Judges; and the Raavan in Sefer Yiraim siman 136; and Rashba in Teshuvot HaRosh HaArukh daf 30b; and the Raah in Sefer HaChinukh mitzvah 464; and Maharshal in Perek HaGah siman 63; and that which Hagahot Perishah wrote — who is there anything etc. — I omitted Rashi and Tosafot there; and Tosafot in the chapter of the slaughterer (Chullin daf 33b) s.v. echad ovdei kochavim, who wrote that here it is not relevant at all to challenge who is there anything etc., since in the end it is forbidden for Israel because of am ha'aretz etc., see there — and this is clear. However, in a dangling limb that is forbidden rabbinically, there are poskim who permit giving it to children of Noah, since there is in it only the mitzvah of separation, as written in siman 55 s.k. 11:"]`,
    '2#ב': `["<b>And flesh that separated, etc.</b> It is implied from the words of the poskim that even if flesh separated from a whole limb, it is only because of flesh in the field treifah:"]`,
    '2#ג': `["<b>Forbidden because of eivar min hachai.</b> So too the Rambam; and the same applies where one cut from flesh inside it — he is liable because of flesh in the field treifah:"]`,
    '3#א': `["<b>If it cannot, etc.</b> But when it can return and live — if the animal was slaughtered, it is permitted, end of his words — Tur and Rambam; and Rashi explained: but if it died, he is not liable for it because of eivar min hachai, but because of nevelah; and so wrote Rav HaMagid there; and it is simple:"]`,
    '3#ב': `["<b>It cannot, etc.</b> And the Tur concluded: and a broken bone — if skin and flesh cover most of it, it is permitted, etc.; and so wrote the Rambam there; and it is implied from their words that at the outset — meaning in a dangling limb — there is no distinction whether skin and flesh cover most of it, but only whether it can return and live or not; and this is specifically in a truly dangling limb — meaning it was uprooted from its place entirely and is connected a bit; but when the limb slipped from its place and is still wrapped in its sinews — this is that which Rav Beit Yosef wrote in the hagahah, and it will be explained shortly (and see above siman 55 s.k. 7):"]`,
    '3#ג': `["<b>Slaughter.</b> And the Rambam wrote there: and if the animal died, we view it as if it fell from life; therefore one receives lashes for it because of eivar min hachai, end of his words; and Rav HaMagid wrote there that he derived this law from the Talmud:"]`,
    '3#ד': `["<b>Forbidden.</b> And Rav HaMagid explained there — because of eivar min hachai, because of the mitzvah of separation rabbinically:"]`,
    '3#ה': `["<b>And see above siman 55.</b> Seif 5 — if it was salted with other flesh, and the rest of its laws were explained there, on the correct understanding:"]`,
    '3#ו': `["<b>The extra portion, etc.</b> See above siman 43 s.k. 9 and siman 54 s.k. 9:"]`,
    '3#ז': `["<b>In a place that does not render treifah.</b> And in a place that does render treifah — see above siman 55 s.k. 7:"]`,
    '3#ח': `["<b>Permitted to eat it, etc.</b> For it is similar to eggs that were plucked — they are permitted, for since they do not rot, there is life-force in them; and this limb that slipped and is still wrapped in its sinews certainly does not rot; and anything that does not rot has life-force in it and is permitted, end of his words — Rashba there; and accordingly it is implied that even if it cannot return and live it is permitted, as is implied in the Talmud regarding eggs that were plucked; and even though in a dangling limb it was explained that whatever cannot return and live is forbidden — that deals with what is not wrapped at all in sinews; nevertheless it is difficult, for Rashba follows his reasoning that he permits eggs that were plucked, as Rav HaMagid and Beit Yosef wrote in his name; but according to what appears correct to us, like the Rambam — eggs that were plucked, Israel practiced to forbid — if so, the same applies to a slipped limb; and so the Rambam wrote explicitly chapter 5 of Forbidden Foods din 7 — and these are his words: a limb slipped, or was crushed, or was flattened, such as eggs that were crushed or ground — this is not forbidden from the Torah, for there is in it a measure of life-force; therefore it does not rot; nevertheless it is forbidden to eat from the custom that all Israel practiced from before, for it resembles eivar min hachai, end of his words; and so wrote Rav Beit Yosef in the hagahah that the custom to be stringent also in this is according to what Darkei Moshe wrote thus in the name of Or Zarua general rule 50 din 11; and in Or Zarua there he brought only the words of the Rambam mentioned above; and it appears that therefore Rav Beit Yosef says it is permitted to eat from the law, like eggs; but the custom is to be stringent also in this, like eggs:"]`,
    '3#ט': `["<b>But the custom is to be stringent also in this.</b> It is implied even if skin and flesh cover most of it; and so is implied in the Rambam there, who afterward wrote: the bone broke — if skin and flesh cover most of it, etc.; it is implied that at the outset, in a slipped limb, there is no distinction whether skin and flesh cover most of it or not; but it is all in the laws of prohibition and permission regarding the case of the impure animal that was slaughtered from its place, before s.v. beheimah shenikra'at bah rov habasar hachof et hakereis etc., he distinguishes also regarding a slipped body whether skin and flesh cover most of it or not, see there; however, all this is in a slipped limb; but in a broken bone — see above siman 55 seif 5 — its laws were explained, for there is its place. And Taz omitted all these laws of slipped limb that Rav Beit Yosef wrote, and inserted other laws of broken bone in their place — and he did not act properly:"]`,
  },
  'turei-zahav': {
    '1#_': `A limb from a living animal. In Tur it is written that it applies only in pure animals — and it is difficult: what practical difference is there, for an impure animal is forbidden in any case; and the Tur is not teaching the law of lashes, but teaches us a practical difference regarding giving to children of Noah — for eivar min hachai is forbidden to him; therefore it is forbidden for Israel to give it to him because of lifnei iver etc.; and he teaches us that this is only in pure animals, but an impure animal may be given to him, for he is not warned regarding this. It is further difficult: why did the Tur need to write that there is a prohibition of eating even less than an olive-bulk, for in all the Torah we hold that half a measure is forbidden from the Torah for eating; and what did he write here that he needed to write it; and one must also explain the expression therefore that the Tur wrote — it has no explanation; apparently also that which he wrote afterward, flesh that separated from a living animal is forbidden — if so, why did he need at first to write regarding a limb that has only flesh that it is forbidden; and it appears to me that the Tur came to warn lest we err to say that here there is no prohibition at all in less than an olive-bulk, from that which Rav said in the Gemara: eivar min hachai requires that there be in it an olive-bulk — what is the reason? Eating is written regarding it; and it appears at first glance that regarding prohibition too he said thus, that in less than an olive-bulk there is no prohibition at all; therefore he teaches us that it refers specifically to lashes; and the main intent of the Tur is to say lest we err to explain that which is written and flesh in the field — the intent is also on the prohibition of eivar min hachai; that is, the verse you shall not eat the soul applies to a limb that has flesh, sinews, and bones; and the verse and flesh in the field applies to a limb that has only flesh; but anything that is not a limb and is flesh has no prohibition at all — it teaches us that we do not explain thus, but from the verse of the soul it is well implied even if there is in it only flesh, for all is included in the first verse; for from that verse we learn prohibition for domesticated animal, undomesticated animal, and fowl, from the reason that the verse deals with every soul; and that it does not apply in impure animals is that the verse excluded them in that verse: whatever its flesh is permitted, its limbs are forbidden, as is in the Gemara; and since we learn from this verse that for all matters he speaks, automatically we know to forbid even in a limb that is flesh alone; and if so, necessarily the verse and flesh in the field applies even if it is not a limb, as explained.`,
    '3#_': `The slipped limb. Meaning: and not broken — it is written in Darkei Moshe — and these are his words: it is written in Or Zarua: a dangling limb is forbidden even if it returned and was bound well, since once it was forbidden once it has no permission again; we are concerned perhaps it did not heal well enough, end of his words; and it does not appear that if we say since it was bound, certainly it did not go outside — for the matter of permitting the animal, as the Tur wrote siman 55 — even though it is a concern of treifah d'oraisa, all the more so we rely on this for the prohibition of a dangling limb, which is rabbinical, end of his words.`,
  },
  'baer-heitev': {
    '1#_': `And fowl. And one receives lashes for it; but it does not apply in fish and locusts.`,
    '2#א': `Limb. In Tur he wrote that it applies only in pure animals and not in impure ones; and Bach wrote that there is a practical difference for us where it is forbidden because of eivar min hachai — it is a complete entity and even in a thousand is not nullified; and in this Shach agreed with him. But that which Bach further wrote that in impure animals it is permitted to give it to children of Noah — he challenged him. However, in a dangling limb there are poskim who permit giving it to children of Noah, since there is in it only the mitzvah of separation alone.`,
    '2#ב': `Forbidden. Even if flesh separated from a whole limb, it is only because of flesh in the field.`,
    '2#ג': `Forbidden. Because of eivar min hachai; and the same applies where one cut from flesh inside it — he is liable because of flesh in the field treifah.`,
    '3#א': `Forbidden. Rav HaMagid explained — because of the mitzvah of separation rabbinically; and Shach wrote that if it can return and live — if the animal was slaughtered, it is permitted; but if it died, he is not liable for it because of eivar min hachai but because of nevelah; and if it cannot return and live and the animal died — the Rambam ruled that we view it as if it fell from life and one receives lashes for it because of eivar min hachai. And in a dangling limb there is no distinction whether skin and flesh cover most of it or not; the distinction is only whether it can return and live — and specifically if it was uprooted from its place entirely and is connected a bit; but when it slipped from its place and is still wrapped in its sinews — this is that which Rav Beit Yosef wrote in the hagahah.`,
    '3#ב': `Above. There siman 55 it was explained — if it was salted with other flesh, what is its law, see there.`,
    '3#ג': `The slipped one. Darkei Moshe wrote in the name of Or Zarua: a dangling limb — even if it returned and was bound well — is forbidden; and in my humble opinion, if we rely for permitting the animal to say that certainly it did not go outside — even though it is a concern of treifot d'oraisa, all the more so one should rely on this in a rabbinical prohibition, end of his words.`,
    '3#ד': `To eat it. For it is similar to eggs that were plucked (for since they do not rot, there is life-force in them; even though in a dangling limb it was explained that if it cannot return and live it is forbidden — there it deals with what is not wrapped at all; but here it is still wrapped in sinews). But the custom is to be stringent even if skin and flesh cover most of it. Shach.`,
  },
  'beer-hagolah': {
    '1#_': `Baraita Chullin daf 101:`,
    '2#א': `Statement of R' Yochanan there daf 102, and he explains there that a limb is forbidden because of you shall not eat the soul with the flesh.`,
    '2#ב': `Mishnah there daf 64:`,
    '3#א': `Baraita daf 73:`,
    '3#ב': `Rambam chapter 5 of Laws of Forbidden Foods; and we learn from the law of male eggs:`,
    '4#_': `Dispute of Rav Ami and Rav Assi daf 93, and like R' Yochanan there.`,
  },
  'beur-hagra': {
    '1#_': `If it cannot, etc. Tosefta chapter 3: a dangling limb in an animal — there is in it to raise a long piece; dangling flesh in an animal — there is in it to raise a long piece; the bone broke and went outside and most of skin and flesh surround it — kosher; and so in Tur; and this is what he said in the hagahah; and see above siman 55 — meaning that in a broken bone the law is not so, but depends on skin and flesh etc.`,
    '2#_': `Even if, etc. Gemara 73b: R' Yosef said take etc.; they said to him: what is the difficulty when they said etc.`,
    '3#_': `The slipped limb, etc. Thus Rashba: we did not hear skin and flesh except in a broken bone; here in a slipped one — no; therefore they did not distinguish in the case of the impure animal slaughtered from its place between skin and flesh etc.; and know that soft eggs are like a slipped limb and are permitted because there is life-force in them, as written in siman 64; and the same for a limb that slipped and is still wrapped in its sinews and does not rot — anything that does not rot has life-force in it, as written regarding eggs; and thus the Rambam chapter 5: a limb slipped etc., such as eggs etc.; and so in the Tosefta mentioned above: dangling limb etc., the bone broke etc.; from which is implied that a dangling limb is one that slipped; and he said it depends on whether there is in it to raise a long piece, and not on skin and flesh; and this is what the Gemara says regarding eggs: there is life-force in them; and even though they said there and you not etc. — because they do not heal, as written there; unlike here, which deals with what can be, as explained.`,
    '4#_': `From the custom, etc. Rambam. For we follow the children of Babylon. Ran.`,
  },
  'kereti': {
    '1#א': `Eivar min hachai — the Rambam's view: a limb that has a bone requires that the limb be with the bone; and if one cut it without the bone, there is in them no prohibition of eivar min hachai, only prohibition of flesh in the field treifah you shall not eat. But a limb that has no bone — such as the tongue and heart and the like — if one cut part of it and there is in it an olive-bulk, one receives lashes because of eivar min hachai; and see what I wrote in Peleti.`,
    '1#ב': `And fowl, domesticated animal, and undomesticated animal — even a child of Noah is warned both regarding a limb and regarding flesh from a living animal; even though Israel is not warned regarding flesh from a living animal, only from these that are treifah — which does not apply to a child of Noah; nevertheless for a child of Noah, flesh and limb from a living animal all come from one verse: only flesh with its soul, its blood you shall not eat, etc.; and see Peleti where I elaborated. But fowl — the Rambam wrote in Laws of Kings that it appears to him that a child of Noah is not executed for it; and presumably there is also no deficiency, for their warning is its death regarding a child of Noah; and if so, it is permitted to give it to a child of Noah, and there is no lifnei iver etc.; and see Peleti what I wrote at length regarding what can be argued against him from the Gemara and what can reconcile him, and proof for his words from the Gemara, see there.`,
    '2#א': `Forbidden. And the Tur wrote that it does not apply in an impure animal; and see Peleti what I wrote in resolving their difficulty: what practical difference, in the end it is forbidden; and what difference to me whether because of eivar min hachai or because of impurity, see there; and if it applies to treifah — see Peleti what I wrote in resolving the words of the Rambam, that which Peri Chadash stood on, and so Lechem Mishneh strained himself; and in any case for practical law it applies also to treifah; and the practical difference is as written regarding impurity.`,
    '2#ב': `And flesh that separated, etc. And according to what we hold — forbidden items nullify one another — if so, if an olive-bulk of kosher flesh mixed with an olive-bulk of flesh from a living animal and an olive-bulk of limb from a living animal, behold it is nullified, for forbidden items nullify; and likewise the reverse: an olive-bulk of treifah and an olive-bulk of kosher flesh and an olive-bulk of flesh from a living animal — it is not nullified, for both are treifah and do not nullify; therefore the Shulchan Aruch was precise: forbidden because of flesh in the field treifah.`,
    '3#א': `Limb and dangling flesh — see Peleti that there is a dispute between the Rambam and authors: according to the Rambam from the Torah it is forbidden; according to other authors rabbinically. And in my humble opinion see Peleti that I wrote: according to those who hold rabbinically, one can say that also in this the rabbis equated it to full eivar min hachai to forbid for a child of Noah; but according to the Rambam, if one cut them into dangling limbs after slaughter, one must forbid for a child of Noah; but if one cut them after it died, it is permitted for a child of Noah; and requires further study.`,
    '3#ב': `The slipped limb — in limb and dangling flesh everything depends on whether it can return and live; but regarding slipped, everything depends on whether skin and flesh cover most of it, so wrote the later authorities; and in truth, if they do not cover most of it, it cannot return and live; and see above siman 57.`,
  },
  peleti: {
    '1#א': `Eivar min hachai, etc. — the Rambam's view: one who plucks from a limb that has no bone, even part of it, is liable; and Peri Chadash wrote that his proof is from that which we say chelev is like eivar min hachai, and the novelty is that the Torah permitted it, etc. And this is not proof, for according to all it is forbidden — it is a case of half a measure; only Raavad holds there are no lashes, and chelev that one thought was for prohibition not for lashes — only for prohibition in general, like half a measure; and also according to the one who says taste is like essence d'oraisa, one receives lashes for it — if so, if a whole limb from a living animal gave taste in a pot, is there no lashes for it? And we do not say to him: you should have cut from the limb, he does not receive lashes — why for its taste would he receive lashes? And necessarily, since according to all it is d'oraisa, it is as if he ate the whole limb whole, as in the baraita; and if so, chelev too one thought is forbidden because of impure animals to forbid their broth and sauce; and requires further study. And see what I wrote above siman 43, see there.`,
    '1#ב': `And fowl — and the Rambam at the end of Laws of Kings wrote that fowl appears that a child of Noah is not executed for it; it teaches that he is not warned from the Torah, for its warning is its death regarding a child of Noah; and see responsa Emunat Shmuel that he also wrote on this; and behold Raavad challenged him; and there is a practical difference for practical law whether it is permitted to give a limb from a living animal from fowl to a child of Noah — whether there is lifnei iver do not place a stumbling block, or not. And behold there is some proof for the Rambam from that which Tosafot challenged Chullin daf 33 s.v. echad nochri etc. — they challenged R' Meir who holds eivar min hachai does not apply in undomesticated animal and in fowl for Israel, but for an idolater it applies — who is there anything forbidden for an idolater and permitted for Israel; and Tosafot answered: nevertheless it is forbidden for Israel because it requires slaughter etc., end of his words; and it is difficult for the Rambam according to the one who says slaughter is not from the Torah — if so, prohibition of limb without slaughter also does not apply; behold he can pluck a limb and eat it, for the commandment and you shall slaughter does not apply to it; and Tosafot's difficulty is in its place; and necessarily it is proven that R' Meir who holds there is slaughter of fowl from the Torah; and it is difficult: if so, that which we learned Chullin daf 28 — flaying with a knife renders garments impure — let the knife help to purify from nevelah status, according to the one who says there is no slaughter for fowl from the Torah; and it is difficult for the one who challenges; necessarily for R' Meir, as Tosafot wrote explicitly there s.v. tehani; and it is difficult: if so, for R' Meir in any case it is compelled from the above that he holds there is slaughter for fowl from the Torah — and what is the difficulty; therefore the Rambam holds that a child of Noah is not commanded regarding eivar min hachai in fowl; and requires further study: who is there anything that is also permitted for children of Noah; and requires further study. And behold one can challenge the Rambam from that which we say Pesachim daf 73: according to the one who disqualifies in a bundle, exempt — that which we learned: one who slaughters a chatat on Shabbat for an idolater and outside — he is liable for three chatat — what did he repair? And Rashi explained: it lacks purification from nevelah status, for idolater's offering defiles in a tent; and they answer: he removes it from eivar min hachai status; and Rashi explained for a child of Noah that if he ate it he is not executed; and in Chullin daf 40 the Gemara proved from that which it did not teach slaughter, that in bird chatat it deals, see there (and see what I wrote siman 4, see there); and it is difficult for the Rambam's views — for behold in fowl there is no warning for a child of Noah at all; and if so, it is still difficult what did he repair; and requires further study. And what appears in its reconciliation is that one should infer from Rashi's explanation why he explained repaired from eivar min hachai for a child of Noah — perhaps plainly for Israel; and if it still did not help, for in any case it is forbidden because of idolater's offering and the like — one can say that in all of them an olive-bulk of flesh is required; but if he ate an olive-bulk of flesh with sinews and bones combined, he does not receive lashes because of other prohibitions, unlike one who eats a limb from a living animal who receives lashes in such a case, as the Rambam wrote and Tosafot explicitly; but when it was slaughtered, he removed it from eivar min hachai status; and if so, one who eats an olive-bulk combined of flesh and its bone and sinews — since there is no olive-bulk of flesh, he is exempt from other prohibitions, and it repaired well; and one need not invoke a child of Noah — one can say Rashi holds in the Gemara's explanation according to the one who disqualifies in a bundle exempt, as Tosafot wrote s.v. according to R' Yehudah — meaning R' Yehudah challenges according to R' Shimon; and according to his view it is difficult what did he repair; and if so, he challenges well what did he repair for eivar min hachai; and one need not invoke as above that we require an olive-bulk, for R' Shimon holds any amount for lashes, and they said olive-bulk only for offering; and if so, even in a minute amount of flesh there are lashes for all prohibitions and it is forbidden in eating and benefit; and if so, it is still difficult what did he repair; therefore Rashi needed to explain repaired regarding a child of Noah; but the Rambam does not hold that the difficulty is for R' Shimon, only the difficulty is for R' Yehudah who even according to his view disqualifies in a bundle, and one need not invoke blood exempt; and if so, one can well say that he repaired from eivar min hachai, for there is no liability on a minute amount of flesh with sinews and bones, and an olive-bulk of flesh is required — and all this is for Israel, and it does not deal with a child of Noah at all; and requires further study. But if it is difficult — this difficulty is from that which we say in the Gemara Chullin daf 101b: eivar min hachai applies in domesticated animal, undomesticated animal, and fowl, both pure and impure, etc.; and R' Yehudah said: both of them from one verse they expounded, as it is written you shall not eat the soul with the flesh; R' Yehudah holds: whatever you are commanded regarding its blood, you are commanded regarding its limbs; and these impure ones — regarding whose blood you are commanded, you are commanded regarding their limbs; and the Gemara challenges R' Yehudah: why do we need a verse — let eivar min hachai prohibition come and apply to impurity prohibition, since its prohibition applies to a child of Noah; and it is difficult: what is the difficulty — for even in fowl eivar min hachai applies, and this is the verse regarding whose blood you are commanded; unlike if not for the verse — only because its prohibition applies to a child of Noah this is not relevant in impure fowl, for a child of Noah is not warned regarding eivar min hachai; and requires further study at first glance. And what appears to me to reconcile in two ways: one way — Tosafot challenged s.v. bo etc., they challenged from the Torah you shall not eat — let eivar min hachai prohibition apply to impurity prohibition, according to the one who holds the prohibition stands for limbs — behold it is prohibition in a single body; and according to all, prohibition applies prohibition; and Tosafot answered that impurity prohibition preceded while it was not yet bound in sinews, see there end of his words; and this is in domesticated animal; but in fowl — behold the Rambam in Forbidden Foods chapter 3 halachah 8 ruled: impure fowl egg in which the chick began to develop inside and she ate it — she receives lashes because of fowl sheretz; behold as long as the chick is inside the egg, there is no prohibition of you shall not eat impure, only prohibition of sheretz; only when it goes out to the air of the world does the prohibition of you shall not eat impure apply; but inside the egg there is prohibition of eating sheretz; and see Kesef Mishneh what he wrote on this; and accordingly, when does impure chick prohibition of impurity apply — when it goes out from the egg to the air, for then the sheretz prohibition flies off and impurity prohibition applies, and it is also called eivar min hachai prohibition, for while alive it stands for limbs; and if so, it is prohibition in a single body — impure and eivar min hachai — and one cannot say prohibition does not apply to prohibition, as Tosafot's difficulty; and Tosafot answered that impurity prohibition did not precede — this is not relevant here, for impurity prohibition applies only from the time of exiting the egg, and then eivar min hachai applies; and if so, a verse is not needed — it is plain that it applies, for it is prohibition in a single body; and necessarily the verse comes for domesticated animal, where single-body is not relevant, as Tosafot wrote — he challenges well the Gemara: behold its prohibition applies to a child of Noah; and why need the prohibition does not stand for limbs; and if so, nevertheless impurity prohibition preceded — if so, it should be difficult for the one who holds the prohibition stands for limbs; and requires further study. And behold Maharsha on daf 103 was inclined that he challenged Resh Lakish who holds eivar min hachai does not apply to treifah because the prohibition does not stand for limbs — if so, why need the verse that eivar min hachai is not in impure, as written soul with flesh, whatever its flesh is permitted etc. — let it be derived that impurity prohibition preceded, and even if the prohibition stands for limbs, impurity prohibition is forbidden first, as Tosafot wrote, see there; and one must understand why he challenged thus to Resh Lakish and not to R' Yehudah who holds the prohibition stands for limbs — for in any case he agrees that impurity prohibition precedes while it was not yet bound in sinews; and if so, it is difficult why need a verse — how can eivar min hachai prohibition apply to preceding impurity prohibition, and they do not have it; and according to this, one can say that the verse was needed for fowl, for impurity prohibition applies only when it exits the egg, and then eivar min hachai prohibition applies, and he teaches us that it does not apply; therefore he challenged Resh Lakish who holds the prohibition does not stand for limbs — if so, in fowl too impurity prohibition precedes; and there is no answer. However, in any case one can reconcile: one can say that that which we say the prohibition does not stand for limbs — we derive from that which the Torah revealed whatever its flesh is permitted etc., that the prohibition does not stand for limbs, and impurity prohibition preceded; and see Tosafot — only the main difficulty of Maharsha, for behold impurity precedes even if the prohibition stands for limbs while not yet bound in sinews — from which is implied that his reasoning is external and one should not derive at all; but according to what is written well, one can derive from fowl that it applies only when it goes out to the light of the world; and requires further study. And one can say that at first glance it is difficult why need a verse — it is plain that eivar min hachai prohibition applies before impurity prohibition, for it is clear that the beginning of the animal's existence is in extreme smallness — it is difficult to depict that in a limb there would be an olive-bulk of flesh alone without sinews and bones; but it is certain that before there is flesh in the limb, there is from the beginning a bit of flesh and a bit of sinews and bones, until in combination they are an olive-bulk; and this testifies to the nature of offspring that before the limb grows and has only an olive-bulk of flesh, there is only a bit of flesh, but in combination with sinews and bone it is an olive-bulk; and if so, eivar min hachai prohibition precedes, for in its smallness it is eivar min hachai, for in combination there is here an olive-bulk of flesh, bone, and sinew; but impurity prohibition does not exist, for there is no olive-bulk of flesh here; and when it grows afterward and has an olive-bulk of flesh, then impurity prohibition applies, and eivar min hachai already applied; and even though nevertheless it is forbidden because of half a measure, since there are no lashes, and because of eivar min hachai prohibition there are lashes — if so, eivar min hachai prohibition preceded, and impurity prohibition will not apply again; and see Ran Tosafot Shevuot 22. And if so, it is difficult why need a verse — for behold eivar min hachai preceded; and it must be said that in truth in such a case it was not needed, only certainly there are large animals that immediately in their existence and formation their limbs were bound in sinews, large limbs, and they have an olive-bulk of flesh, like large rams and elephants; and one might have thought impurity preceded, for it applies immediately; therefore a verse was needed: whatever you are commanded regarding its blood, you are commanded regarding its limbs, etc.; and eivar min hachai applies also in this. And this is in domesticated animal; but in fowl born from the egg, it is impossible that in one of the many limbs there would be an olive-bulk of flesh without bone and sinew — go and consider how much strength this chick has until the egg contains it and wraps it (and possibly the bar yuchni egg that Chazal spoke of in riddles); and most limbs are only in combination of flesh with bones and sinews, before there is an olive-bulk of flesh; and if so, eivar min hachai prohibition preceded; and accordingly on the end, a verse is not needed — certainly eivar min hachai preceded; the verse is only for large animals; and he challenges well: behold its prohibition applies to a child of Noah; and requires further study. In this too the words of Maharsha mentioned above that he challenged why need the verse that its flesh is forbidden — let it be derived prohibition does not apply to prohibition; one can say it was needed in such a case where the limb does not hold an olive-bulk of flesh, only flesh, sinews, and bones; and as written, prohibition does not apply to prohibition is not relevant — if so, still how do we derive from one whose flesh is forbidden, for behold also in this its flesh is entirely permitted; and what practical difference whether it became forbidden from its species, only this is for Resh Lakish, but for R' Yehudah requires further study, for half a measure is forbidden from the Torah, therefore he did not challenge from Resh Lakish who holds half a measure is permitted. And in this too is reconciled that which Rashi wrote daf 103 that eivar min hachai came and applied to treifah because of miggo that it applies to a child of Noah — and he did not write because of miggo that it applies to limbs that do not have an olive-bulk of flesh, only in combination of sinews and bones it applies to all limbs, even very large ones, for Rashi holds that anonymously it teaches even in very large rams where there is no limb in which there would not be an olive-bulk of flesh. And in this is reconciled that which Tosafot wrote for Rava who said: he plucked a limb and rendered it treifah — from this we learn he holds the prohibition does not stand for limbs; if so, that which we say he plucked chelev from treifah, he is liable for three — how do you find it, for how can eivar min hachai apply to treifah; and why need inclusive prohibition, for Rava does not hold otherwise, for if so, why does he require specifically that it became treifah in it; and according to what is written it is well: this inclusive prohibition that applies to a child of Noah — Rava does not hold; but this inclusive prohibition he holds from miggo that it applies to a limb that does not have an olive-bulk of flesh, it applies also to all limbs; and Rava does not establish it that it deals in one animal, only in two, and there is no inclusive prohibition; but that which we say he plucked from treifah etc. — it deals in one animal, and he has inclusive prohibition, as written; and requires further study.`,
    '2#א': `And flesh that separated from a living animal — in Laws of Kings the Rambam ruled that also for a child of Noah flesh that separated from a living animal is forbidden; and Kesef Mishneh wondered: behold its prohibition is from the verse flesh in the field treifah; until they said one who eats eivar min hachai and flesh from a living animal receives two sets of lashes; and if so, what is the nature of a child of Noah for this prohibition of treifah — is a child of Noah commanded regarding treifah, see there; and even though his words are correct, nevertheless the difficulty is not on the Rambam but on the Gemara, for R' Yehudah derives it for him from this verse flesh in the field, etc.; and this is that a child of Noah is forbidden in flesh that separated from a living animal, as is explicit in the chapter of the hide and the sauce explicitly; and if so, the difficulty is on R' Yehudah from this baraita of hide and sauce. Therefore one must reconcile the words of the Gemara plainly — and it is certainly the plain meaning of the verse you shall not eat soul with flesh — it teaches regarding flesh from a living animal, for who mentions a limb; therefore R' Yehudah holds this prohibition of a child of Noah that is written in it flesh with its soul, its blood you shall not eat — we say it comes on what is reasonable regarding eivar min hachai according to the plain meaning of the verse, for flesh in the field is not written in it treifah; and what relevance does a child of Noah have to treifah; and if you say if so, from where do we know eivar min hachai — one can say: since flesh from a living animal was forbidden, eivar min hachai in which there is flesh, sinews, and bones is plainly forbidden, and we learn two things; and if flesh from a living animal requires an olive-bulk of flesh, and eivar min hachai is a minute amount of flesh with sinews and bones — this applies to Israel who requires an olive-bulk; but a child of Noah who is in a minute amount, as the Rambam wrote that measures were stated only for Israel — if so, a minute amount of flesh is also included in flesh from a living animal; therefore for a child of Noah flesh from a living animal and limb from a living animal come from one verse, as written; but for Israel one cannot derive limb from flesh, for in this an olive-bulk is required and in this not — therefore the Torah specified a separate prohibition flesh in the field, etc.; and one comes for eivar min hachai and one for flesh — unlike a child of Noah, one need not derive limb from flesh; and requires further study. In this one can say that R' Yehudah derives flesh from a living animal from flesh in the field treifah, and Rashi explained he derives from you shall not eat soul with flesh, etc.; they go according to their views: for Resh Lakish the above difficulty is difficult — from where do we forbid both for a child of Noah, limb and flesh; and if as above R' Yehudah does not hold that measures were not stated for a child of Noah — for Resh Lakish holds half a measure is permitted from the Torah; and it is difficult: who is there anything forbidden for an idolater in half a measure and permitted for Israel — for Resh Lakish entirely; and necessarily measures were also stated for a child of Noah; and if so, the above difficulty is in its place — from where do we know limb and flesh for a child of Noah; and necessarily it must be said one verse flesh with its soul, etc. includes both limb and flesh; and so for Israel — unlike R' Yehudah who does not hold measures were stated for a child of Noah, and the difficulty who is there anything does not apply — for for Israel in any case half a measure is forbidden; and if so, one can well say as above that for a child of Noah one verse suffices for flesh and for limb, and for Israel two verses, as above. (And behold Mishneh Lamelech in Laws of Chametz and Laws of Oaths debated the words of Raavan whose view is that plain eating is any amount, only tradition extracted that liability is only in an olive-bulk; and he wondered on him and debated him; and also Mishneh Lamelech; and from the above it is proven that if plain eating is in an olive-bulk, how can there be for a child of Noah in any amount — behold eating is written in the verse, and plain eating is in an olive-bulk; and necessarily plain eating is in a minute amount, only Mishneh Lamelech extracted it; and regarding a child of Noah where there is no tradition in a minute amount (and see Mishneh Lamelech that he himself admits for Resh Lakish it does not exist, and plain eating is in an olive-bulk; and if so, it is plain that he holds that also for a child of Noah an olive-bulk is required, for eating is stated in it; and requires further study). And afterward I found in the chapter of the hide and the sauce that Rashba brought this difficulty in the name of Ramban: how is the idolater liable for flesh from a living animal that is derived for R' Yehudah from flesh in the field treifah, etc.; and he answered that the verse stated regarding a child of Noah includes both limb and flesh; but the verse stated regarding Israel includes only limb and not flesh, see there. And behold one can challenge that which they challenge at the beginning of chapter 2 of Pesachim on R' Eliezer who said you shall not eat — even prohibition of benefit is implied; and behold eivar min hachai that is written you shall not eat, etc. — and from where that a person should not give eivar min hachai to an idolater, as it is said lifnei iver do not place a stumbling block; the reason is lifnei iver is forbidden, but otherwise it is permitted; and it is forbidden in benefit, etc.; and it is difficult: perhaps it deals with flesh from a living animal; or we derive from flesh in the field treifah you shall not eat — this is prohibition of benefit is not implied, for treifah the Merciful permitted to dogs you shall cast it; but for a child of Noah it is forbidden as above, and there is lifnei iver, etc.; and if he cited because of eivar min hachai and did not cite flesh from a living animal — this is borrowed language and inclusive, as written in tractate Sanhedrin: a child of Noah is commanded regarding eivar min hachai and counted it among seven commandments, and does not specify flesh from a living animal, for all is included in eivar min hachai (and perhaps one can reject: since all concern is in flesh from a living animal, for even its benefit is forbidden, it was not appropriate to be concise; and it is strained). And behold one can reconcile this difficulty from the Gemara of Pesachim (and see similarly that he labored to give a reason why measures were not stated for a child of Noah — and it is not needed, the matter is simple, for this half measure was included for R' Yochanan from the verse whoever eats chelev, and this was not stated for a child of Noah; and so the language of the Rambam, for measures were given only to Israel, teaches thus; and the matter is as written, for plain eating means a minute amount); and according to what I wrote, therefore a child of Noah is liable for flesh because the plain meaning of the verse is reasonable on flesh; and that which we do not say so for Israel — for a verse for flesh from a living animal was not needed, it is already derived from the inclusive rule of flesh in the field, etc.; if so, it is difficult: these that there is no difference between the prohibition flesh in the field treifah, etc., and the prohibition you shall not eat soul with flesh; but if we say you shall not eat — prohibition of benefit is implied, but in treifah only prohibition of eating, as written you shall cast to dogs, etc.; if so, one can say forever you shall not eat soul applies to flesh from a living animal like a child of Noah; and if it is difficult: behold we already derived from flesh in the field treifah — this is not difficult, the intent of the Torah was to forbid it in benefit, and therefore it wrote you shall not eat the soul with the flesh, and prohibition of benefit is implied; but from this verse flesh in the field, etc., it is only prohibition of eating like other treifot; and the difficulty is in its place: perhaps you shall not eat applies only to flesh from a living animal; and necessarily according to his view this inclusive rule does not exist, that the plain meaning of the verse is more reasonable to explain on flesh than what we explained on flesh; and on the contrary on limb and not on flesh; and not like the baraita of hide and sauce. And if so, it comes out well that according to his view the plain meaning of the verse is more reasonable to explain on limb than what we explained on flesh; and accordingly even for a child of Noah one can say so (this is all according to what is written, and not like Ramban); and in truth one must say according to his view that children of Noah are permitted in flesh that separated, for there is no verse here; and it comes out well according to his view that you shall not eat for R' Eliezer — eivar min hachai is forbidden in benefit; if so, it is proven from what is written that this verse you shall not eat soul with flesh is the plain meaning of the verse on eivar min hachai; and this verse flesh in the field is on flesh from a living animal — there is no prohibition of flesh from a living animal for a child of Noah; and R' Eliezer does not hold this baraita in the chapter of hide and sauce; and the Gemara challenges well: why need flesh from a living animal, for it is not relevant for lifnei iver, for it is not stated for a child of Noah; and the Gemara answers: eivar min hachai is permitted in benefit; and if so, we return to the above that flesh from a living animal is more reasonable only for Israel we derive from and flesh in the field, etc.; and the law is true law: flesh from a living animal is forbidden for a child of Noah; and requires further study. And by way of sharpening for students I would say on this difficulty that we challenged above, Laws of slaughter siman 27 — why does the Gemara challenge in Pesachim, and behold eivar min hachai, etc. — perhaps R' Natan who said from where that one should not give, etc., holds like the Sages that it does not apply in impurity; and this is for Israel; but for a child of Noah it is forbidden, as Rav's statement there; and if so, R' Natan deals with eivar min hachai of impure animal that Israel was not commanded at all, all the more so there is no prohibition of benefit; but for a child of Noah it is forbidden to give because of lifnei iver, for a child of Noah is commanded regarding it; and see what I wrote in its reconciliation; and one can say that he holds this that an idolater is forbidden in eivar min hachai of impure animal is not in the chapter of the hide — this one who holds still convulsing is permitted for a child of Noah even though slaughter is not relevant, because of the reason who is there anything; see there Gemara and Tosafot Chullin daf 28 what they wrote on this. If so, the same applies in impurity; and that which Tosafot wrote nevertheless for Israel it is forbidden because of impurity — the Gemara strains: what relevance does this have to that; in the end regarding this eivar min hachai prohibition it is relevant who is there anything. But all this is if the Torah's decree whoever its flesh is permitted etc. — eivar min hachai does not apply in Israel in impurity; if so, one can well say the same for a child of Noah who is there anything; but if the reason is because prohibition does not apply to prohibition — if so, for a child of Noah where this is not relevant, for he has no impurity prohibition, it is plain that it applies; and as we say this is the reason he gave; and if so, there is no answer that according to his view who holds there is prohibition of benefit in eivar min hachai — if so, a reason is needed why eivar min hachai does not apply to impurity, for there is no because prohibition does not apply to prohibition, for there is an additional prohibition from miggo that it applies to prohibition of benefit, etc., that impurity is permitted in benefit, and additional prohibition — according to all prohibition applies to prohibition; and it must be said the Torah's decree lest eivar min hachai apply in impurity whose flesh is forbidden; and if so, also for a child of Noah it is not forbidden who is there anything, etc.; and the Gemara challenges well from that which one should not give, etc. — for according to his view, eivar min hachai of impurity is not relevant for a child of Noah like for Israel; but according to the conclusion that it is permitted in benefit — if so, there is no additional prohibition; and if so, prohibition does not apply to prohibition — one can well say for a child of Noah even in impurity eivar min hachai applies, and who is there anything does not apply; and for Israel the reason is because prohibition does not apply to prohibition; and there is no answer; and requires further study.`,
    '2#ב': `From a living animal. And Tur wrote that it does not apply in impure animals; and Bach wondered: what practical difference for practical law — in the end it is forbidden because of impurity; and see what the later authorities wrote. And if one says the practical difference is whether one receives lashes or prohibition in general — regarding disqualification of testimony, as explained in Choshen Mishpat siman 34, see there; requires further study: if one ate a limb in which in combination of flesh, bone, and sinew there is only an olive-bulk, he does not receive lashes because of impurity, but because of eivar min hachai he receives lashes and is disqualified for testimony — a great practical difference. Also if he swore he will not eat this limb and there is in it a minute amount of flesh, etc. — it applies to impurity, for an oath applies to half a measure; and see below siman 238; and that which Shach wrote the practical difference regarding a sick person whether they need to release the oath — but if eivar min hachai applies, behold he swore to uphold a d'oraisa matter and it does not apply at all, and release is not needed, for eivar min hachai applies in impurity. However, in any case one can say: an apostate Israelite — it is common regarding limbs of impure animal; but it is not common regarding limbs from a living animal; and it is known that lifnei iver prohibition is only where apart from this it is not common regarding the prohibition; and as we say it stands on two sides of the river; and if so, if it is forbidden only because of impurity, it is permitted to give to an apostate; and regarding lifnei iver there is nothing in it, for impure limbs are common regarding it, and there is no lifnei iver in it; but if they are also forbidden because of eivar min hachai — this is not common regarding the apostate, and it is forbidden to give him because of lifnei iver, etc.; and a great practical difference; and easy negotiation.`,
    '2#ג': `Forbidden — if the animal is treifah, if eivar min hachai applies to treifah (and practical difference as I wrote above regarding apostate and the like) — we say in Chullin daf 33: one who ate eivar min hachai and from treifah — R' Yehudah said he is liable for two; and Resh Lakish said he is liable only for one; and Rava established it like his view against Abbaye, who deals with one who plucked a limb and rendered it treifah in it; and the animal — the prohibition does not stand for limbs; and if so, treifah prohibition and limb prohibition come together; and Resh Lakish holds the prohibition stands for limbs, and treifah prohibition did not come and apply to eivar min hachai; and afterward the Gemara concluded in the name of R' Yochanan: he plucked chelev from treifah and it lived and he ate it — he receives three lashes; and the Gemara was divided on the reason of the matter — whether because the prohibition stands for limbs and all three applied as one; and it deals with that it became treifah immediately at birth; or the prohibition does not stand for limbs, and eivar min hachai prohibition applied to treifah and chelev; and see there another establishment. And behold the Rambam in Forbidden Foods chapter 5 halachah 8 wrote: one who plucked a limb from a living animal and rendered it treifah in removing it and ate it — he is liable for two because of eivar min hachai and because of treifah, for two prohibitions came as one; from this it is implied that the Rambam holds the prohibition does not stand for limbs; and eivar min hachai is not considered an inclusive prohibition, since it applies to a child of Noah; for otherwise how did they apply as one — behold eivar min hachai applied immediately at birth; and also if eivar min hachai were an inclusive prohibition, what of it that it was treifah from before — behold it would be an inclusive prohibition; and also at first glance it is astonishing, for the Rambam himself holds inclusive prohibition; and it is difficult like Tosafot's difficulty on R' Yochanan; and also afterward the Rambam wrote: one who plucks chelev and ate it receives two lashes because of chelev and because of eivar min hachai (and this is difficult, since the prohibition does not stand for limbs, chelev prohibition preceded from exiting to the air of the world — how can eivar min hachai prohibitions apply, behold I have no inclusive prohibition either); he plucked chelev from treifah and ate it — he receives three lashes; necessarily; and this is more difficult, since the prohibition does not stand for limbs, how can eivar min hachai prohibition apply to chelev and treifah, for behold I have no inclusive prohibition either, and treifah in it was not mentioned. And already Lechem Mishneh and authors stood on it; and they reconciled strainedly, as Peri Chadash wrote; and Peri Chadash also explained strainedly, and understanding is difficult. And what appears to me: it is certainly difficult how Rava can say to R' Yehudah if not eivar min hachai and treifah prohibitions come as one — he is not liable for limb and for treifah; and afterward R' Yochanan stated simply that he is liable for limb and treifah; therefore it appears that this is the Rambam's view: for there to be inclusive prohibition because it applies to a child of Noah — we do not say as I wrote above that a prohibition stated regarding children of Noah is not a prohibition for which Israel is liable; and he holds that which we say the animal while alive stands for limbs — and Rashi explained: to cut it into pieces; and one must explain how it stands to cut it — for if one cuts it, the pieces are forbidden because of eivar min hachai; and from the Torah you shall destroy the animal — and it must be said that thus are the matters: were it not that the Torah forbade because of eivar min hachai, the animal would stand to be cut as he wishes, only the Torah forbade because of eivar min hachai and it is impossible to cut it; therefore on every piece and piece eivar min hachai applies lest he cut it and cut from it; and the Rambam holds this applies when other pieces and limbs in the body stand to be cut, were it not for the Merciful One's prohibition of eivar min hachai; but a limb that renders treifah in it in the body — such as thigh and the like — if so, how can it be thought to cut — who is crazy and foolish who would cut a limb and render treifah thereby the whole animal. And this is certainly not for cutting and limbs remain; and this is what Rava innovated: certainly in general the prohibition stands for limbs and treifah prohibition did not come and apply; but in one who cuts a limb that renders treifah in it — this is certainly the prohibition does not stand for limbs, and treifah prohibition applied, for it is prohibition in a single body; and this is what R' Yehudah holds in such a case the prohibition does not stand for limbs; and if so, now that we were informed of a limb that renders treifah in it, such as thigh and the like — the prohibition does not stand for limbs; and if so, an animal that became treifah — all such limbs, treifah prohibition applied to them, for they did not stand for limbs; and at the time of treifah it applied to them, for then he can pluck them; for otherwise the animal is treifah; and if so, they come together in Rava's matter, and it applied to them; and if so, even on other limbs where there is no treifah — it depends; and even though eivar min hachai preceded in other limbs that stand for limbs — nevertheless treifah prohibition applied from miggo that it applies to limbs where treifah depends, like thigh, the like, etc. — it applies to all limbs of the animal inclusively; and it is an inclusive prohibition; if so, Rava who innovated this for us in limbs that became treifah in it — it is prohibition in a single body, and it applied from this; we learn the other of R' Yochanan that treifah applies to eivar min hachai in every death and in every manner it became treifah, from the reasoning of miggo, etc.; and one was stated from its associate — and examine, for it is correct and deep; and these are the words of the Rambam who summarized in one statement: he plucked a limb and rendered treifah — for it is prohibition in a single body; and he taught us this in that limb and the like the prohibition does not stand for limbs; and inclusive prohibition was innovated for us for all limbs; therefore he afterward wrote that he is liable for three, for treifah applied to the limb; and examine, for it is correct. And behold one should infer: why does treifah prohibition not apply to eivar min hachai — and we do not say from miggo that it applies to chelev in an animal, for chelev of treifah is forbidden, it should apply also to the animal itself, and it would be inclusive prohibition. And it is possible that therefore the Rambam holds chelev of treifah has no prohibition of negative commandment, only prohibition in general; and if so, miggo that it applies to chelev is not relevant, for it applied only to prohibition in general; and we require that prohibition of treifah negative commandment apply to eivar min hachai — and this does not exist. If one says in found treifah chelev found in the animal's innards — this is not nullified by the rule of treifah, only chelev that was formed afterward; and this is not because of the reason of treifah, only it is a species of animal and forbidden matter, and resembles broth and its sauce; but in eivar min hachai their broth and sauce are forbidden; but this is strained, for in eivar min hachai we do not forbid chelev since there is permission for eivar min hachai; and if so, there should still be miggo that it applies to chelev, for what is not relevant in eivar min hachai — since there is permission for its prohibition; and it is clear as Rosh wrote: in any case Taz wrote above in Laws of slaughter siman 24: that which we say in the Gemara chelev is permitted in eivar min hachai, for there is permission for its prohibition — and Taz explained: this means eivar min hachai prohibition is light in itself, since there is permission for its prohibition; it is not implied from this sugya; for if so, it would be appropriate for treifah prohibition to apply to eivar min hachai prohibition in a severe prohibition. However, this is difficult according to what Tosafot wrote in Yevamot chapter 2: one who comes upon his brother's wife and his wife's sister — they thought for R' Shimon he is liable because of his brother's wife, for it is not prohibition in a single body, for his sister's prohibition has permission for its prohibition when his wife dies, unlike his brother's wife where there is no permission, see there in Tosafot; and if so, that which the Gemara said why need the verse that eivar min hachai applies in impurity — let it be derived that its prohibition applies to a child of Noah; and the Gemara answers for R' Yehudah: so too it was needed only for R' Eliezer; and they further challenged: even for R' Eliezer according to the one who holds the prohibition stands for limbs — behold it is prohibition in a single body, see there; and it is difficult: perhaps R' Eliezer holds like R' Shimon, and eivar min hachai has permission for its prohibition in slaughter, unlike impurity; and if so, it is not prohibition in a single body, and impurity prohibition preceded; and requires further study.`,
    '3#_': `Limb or flesh, etc. — in the Gemara we say that there is in it only the mitzvah of separation; and according to the Rambam, this means there are no lashes, but there is d'oraisa prohibition; and Mishneh Lamelech gave the reason like half a measure that was included from kol, and nevertheless it was included only for prohibition and not for lashes; and Peri Chadash rejected: since there is no measure, eating is not relevant, therefore there are no lashes; and Mishneh Lamelech's words are correct: let it be resolved half a measure — behold according to the one who says it is a creature by itself, it was also included in Yoma from kol, and there is in it only prohibition and not lashes — what will Peri Chadash and Mishneh Lamelech say on this; one of them took it; only we learn that it was included for prohibition and not for lashes; and the same applies to dangling limb. And the view of Tosafot and Rashi and most commentators: their view is mitzvah of separation — forbidden rabbinically to separate from d'oraisa prohibition; therefore; but d'oraisa prohibition does not exist in them; and Peri Chadash brought for the Rambam's words proof from Tosafot Avodah Zarah that teaches dangling limb and flesh are forbidden for Israel and for a child of Noah — and if it is a rabbinical commandment, what is its nature to forbid a child of Noah; but one must understand this mitzvah of separation — we derive in the Gemara from the extension of flesh in the field treifah you shall not eat; and what relevance does a child of Noah have to this verse flesh in the field treifah; and see what I wrote that we stood in the name of Kesef Mishneh and Rashba on flesh that separated from a living animal that is born from flesh in the field treifah — what relevance does a child of Noah have to it; and all that I wrote in its reconciliation does not apply here; therefore it appears according to Rashi's explanation that the rabbis forbade limb and dangling flesh, and they relied on a verse — one can say that its prohibition they equated to full eivar min hachai, that also for a child of Noah it should be forbidden so there would not be a difference between it and full eivar min hachai. And this reasoning several authors wrote; see above siman 55; and for the Rambam it must deal with that he cut into dangling limbs in the animal beforehand, and he cut them after slaughter, and the verse was extended to forbid them; and for a child of Noah they have no slaughter — forbidden also, for who will permit them; and even for the one who permits still convulsing because of who is there anything — but in this even for Israel it is forbidden; and examine, for it is correct; I elaborated a bit here since the Shulchan Aruch abbreviated, and the laws themselves of Torah — I said it is good to elaborate a bit and to awaken the ear of learners.`,
  },
  'pitchei-teshuva': {
    '1#_': `Forbidden. Peri Megadim in Mishbetzot — what he wrote regarding giving eivar min hachai to a child of Noah; and see on this in responsa Penei Aryeh siman 74 and responsa Zichron Yitzchak siman 108; and see in responsa Beit Efrayim in the introduction to the Yoreh De'ah section what he wrote on this. [And see in responsa Chatam Sofer siman 19 that he was aroused on a new matter according to what is explained in Tosafot Chullin 64a s.v. she'im rakamah regarding eggs — it is a novelty that there is nothing that comes from a living animal and is permitted; if so, it appears one should forbid eggs for a child of Noah in Talmudic times — for regarding Israel the Torah revealed, and not for a child of Noah, like still convulsing that is permitted for Israel and not for a child of Noah; and the Rambam holds chapter 9 of Laws of Kings that even a pure animal fit for Israel — still convulsing is forbidden for a child of Noah, and he has no who is there anything. And even for those who disagree and hold who is there anything — nevertheless in treifah and impurity that are forbidden in any case for Israel, they are forbidden for a child of Noah; and if so, here too eggs are forbidden for a child of Noah — at least treifah eggs; and according to the Rambam all eggs are forbidden. And he wrote that accordingly it is astonishing how they were accustomed in Talmudic times to sell eggs to an idolater, whereas it is explicitly permitted in the Talmud and poskim; see below end of seif 6; and even though one can say because it is common for them in any case etc., nevertheless one should wonder why they give them for free — and this is forbidden even where it is common for them, as is proven from what Tosafot wrote Pesachim 92; however, whole eggs are plainly permitted to give them as a gift, since there is room to suspend that one will seat fowl on them to raise chicks — one fixes them for that purpose, it is permitted; for in anything where there is room to suspend, we rely, as explained in mishnah 8 chapter 5 of Sheviit; and also to give them live treifah eggs one can permit, for perhaps he does not fix them for eating but for work, in the manner of doing many works with treifah eggs, like the work of glue and the like. However, to invite them for foods kneaded with eggs (according to one who holds thus, and according to one who holds thus for the Rambam even in fit animals; and according to those who disagree, in treifot) or that were cooked with eggs — from where do we permit? And one cannot say taste is like essence is not forbidden for a child of Noah — on the contrary, for a child of Noah even in a thousand it is not nullified, and even taste for detriment is forbidden for them; and he left it requiring further study, see there where he elaborated on this.]`,
    '2#_': `In domesticated animal. In Shelah daf 303 he wrote that an animal created through Sefer Yetzirah, as we find in the Gemara that he created a third-grown calf every erev Shabbat — it is permitted to eat it while still alive; and this was the deed of the tribes that Yosef brought, that their daughter grazed to their father, that they ate eivar min hachai, for Yosef thought it was a complete animal, see there; and it appears that nevertheless it is forbidden to eat it without slaughter rabbinically because of marit ayin, as above siman 13 regarding ben peku'ah nine months old that spread over the ground; and one applies all the laws stated there. And according to what is written there in the name of Perishat Derekhim that slaughter is not required, but piercing is required; and if not, it is forbidden because of you shall not eat upon the blood, see there — automatically the same law applies here; and accordingly it is difficult to understand the words of the author of Shelah mentioned above in what he wrote and this was the deed of the tribes.`,
    '3#_': `The separated limb. See Bach; and see in Shach that the reason for his challenge on Bach is that we hold that for idolaters it applies also in impurity; and if so, it is forbidden to give it to them; and see in responsa Emunat Shmuel siman 14 that he wrote to reconcile their words; and he made a new principle: where it is permitted for him and he forbids only for a child of Noah — it is permitted to give, for it is not similar to giving a cup of wine to a nazir, for even though for him it is permitted, nevertheless the Torah forbade him when he becomes a nazir; but in a prohibition that is permitted for him, like eivar min hachai in impure animals — it is permitted to give to a child of Noah, and he is not warned regarding lifnei iver; and that which he cited in separation the language who is there anything — it refers to the prohibition of giving, thus end of his words, see there. And at first glance there is proof for his words from Pesachim daf 22: and behold eivar min hachai etc. — behold for dogs it is permitted, see there; and see in Tosafot there and in Shach above siman 27; and requires further study; and nevertheless for practical law requires further study.`,
  },
  'kaf-hachayim': {
    '1#_': `(1) [Seif 1] A limb from a living animal is forbidden, etc. — You shall not eat the soul with the flesh — this is eivar min hachai; and flesh in the field treifah you shall not eat — this is flesh from a living animal — Chullin 102b; and Rashi explained there that a live limb is called soul, for if you cut it, it does not make exchanges to return again, like a soul that was taken that does not return; and so is implied: you shall not eat the soul while it is still with the flesh — while there is life with the flesh; and flesh in the field implies that it separated from its place — this is flesh from a living animal, end of his words; and Beit Yosef.`,
    '2#_': `(2) There. A limb from a living animal is forbidden, etc. — and one receives lashes for it. Shach s.k. 1. Peri Chadash ot 1; Lechem HaPanim ot 1; Arukh HaShulchan ot 1; and the practical difference whether one receives lashes for it — see above siman 16 end of ot 55, see there; and another practical difference regarding repair; and see below ot 9.`,
    '3#_': `(3) There. Forbidden in domesticated animal, undomesticated animal, and fowl; but not in fish and locusts. Shach s.k. 2. Knesset HaGedolah in Hagahot Taz ot 12; Peri Chadash ot 2; Lechem HaPanim ot 2; Shaarei De'ah ot 2; Chochmat Adam general rule 27 ot 1; Beit Yosef BeShulchan Aruch HaZahav ot 2; Zivchei Tzedek ot 1; and see above siman 13 seif 1 in the hagahah and our words there, with Heaven's help.`,
    '4#_': `(4) There. Forbidden in domesticated animal, undomesticated animal, and fowl — the pure, but not the impure. Rambam beginning of chapter 5 of Forbidden Foods; Tur; Beit Yosef; Bach; Taz s.k. 1; Shach s.k. 3; Peri Chadash ot 3; Shulchan Gavoah ot 1; and nevertheless it is forbidden to give children of Noah eivar min hachai even from impure animals, because for them impure animal is also permitted, and it is forbidden to eat from it eivar min hachai; and it is forbidden to give them because of lifnei iver etc. — Shach there in the name of the Talmud; and several poskim not like Bach, see there; and so Peri Chadash there to forbid and to reject the words of those who permit, see there; and so Machberet ot 1 to reject and to uphold the words of Peri Chadash, see there; and so Arukh HaShulchan ot 4; Chochmat Adam there; Beit Yosef BeShulchan Aruch HaZahav there.`,
  },
  'nekudot-hakesef': {
    '1#_': `(Siman 62 in Taz s.k. 1) And there are those who reconcile, etc. — regarding giving to children of Noah etc. — this is a mistake in a baraita, as Shach wrote s.k. 3, see there; however, that which Shach wrote there at the end of the aforementioned s.k., and as written in this siman s.k. 11 (so it is in Nekudot Yosef) — it is a printing error, and it should read as written in siman 55 s.k. 11.`,
  },
  'yad-avraham': {
    '1#_': `(Siman 62 Shach s.k. 3) And see that in pure animals, where there is eivar min hachai, it is forbidden to give it to children of Noah, etc. Peri Chadash disagrees with what is written in responsa Emunat Shmuel that one does not transgress lifnei iver when he himself is not forbidden in that thing; and so Taz, as Peri Megadim wrote; and so is proven in Tosafot Bava Metzia (daf 10b); and greater than this is proven in Tosafot Chagigah (14) that even where there is a commandment regarding the act, nevertheless lifnei iver applies when his act is for one forbidden in it — such as teaching Torah to an idolater; and so one can learn from what Tosafot wrote in Ketubot (beginning daf 40) that otherwise it should have said that a verse was needed to say that there is upon him prohibition for the one who marries her. I again found that he sensed from Tosafot Chagigah in Sefer Shoshanat HaAmakim general rule 10; and he explained in the name of responsa Bnei Chayya that he distinguishes between that case of handing it to him and between when he feeds him by hand and teaches him, as feeding him. And Rav Shoshanat HaEtz wrote that he did not know from where this distinction. And in my humble opinion, in this there is no sugya of Pesachim (daf 22) that does not establish it in impure and still convulsing, and in such a case where there is no eivar min hachai in Israel and there is for a child of Noah — only we learn that in this there is no matter of giving; and accordingly there is no also what the Tur Bet Shmuel wrote on responsa Emunat Shmuel from the Gemara Chullin (daf 33) that one does not invite an idolater for innards, even though it is permitted for Israel — one can say that inviting to his house is like feeding him by hand.<br><b>And in our case</b> — one who feeds his fellow what is permitted according to the view of the feeder — Chavot Yair wrote in Sukkah (daf 10b) from the incident of Rav Chisda and Rabbah bar Rav Huna, who there came to the house of the Nasi; they reclined R' Nachman in a sukkah set apart from it by four handbreadths; we learn from there that one who feeds his fellow what is permitted according to the view of the feeder — there is no lifnei iver in this, even though he knows that according to his fellow's view it is forbidden — if the feeder is a halachic authority. And there are those who disagree that specifically when the matter is recognizable to his fellow; but when it is not recognizable — no; and so is proven in the chapter Kol HaBasar. And nevertheless, where the halachah was decided not like his view, whether for himself or for others — it is forbidden.`,
  },
  'rabbi-akiva-eiger-yd': {
    '1#_': `(Siman 62 Shach s.k. 3) And his words are correct in this. And see in Teshuvot Beit Hillel (siman 137); and see in Tiferet Lemoshe (below siman 100).`,
    '2#_': `(In another place) See there, and this is clear. See in Sefer Teivat Guma of the Gaon Porat Yosef z"l, parashat Shemot ot 1: a child of Noah who is in danger — if it is permitted for Israel to give him eivar min hachai to heal him; one can say push aside in them and do not die in them was said regarding Israel; but for a child of Noah it was not permitted in a case of saving life, and there is lifnei iver.`,
  },
};
